> It's worth pointing out that Private-Network Access (formerly known as CORS-RFC1918) [is now on hold](https://developer.chrome.com/blog/pna-on-hold). See also [this more informal announcement](https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/g/blink-dev/c/NCV3anf1KtU/m/WyL9rKjtAQAJ) by Google's @christhompson.

@jub0bs thanks for the heads up! That's a shame about PNA, but I believe this feature has independent value even if PNA doesn't happen, and also happens to be much more widely applicable.

For people watching this issue, I expect to have the HTML spec PR up within a week. The related Fetch spec PR is linked above, from the end of last year.

To add to jlunman's point.

> A REST API must not define fixed resource names or hierarchies (an obvious coupling of client and server). Servers must have the freedom to control their own namespace. Instead, allow servers to instruct clients on how to construct appropriate URIs, such as is done in HTML forms and URI templates, by defining those instructions within media types and link relations. [Failure here implies that clients are assuming a resource structure due to out-of band information, such as a domain-specific standard, which is the data-oriented equivalent to RPC’s functional coupling]. [https://roy.gbiv.com/untangled/2008/rest-apis-must-be-hypertext-driven](https://roy.gbiv.com/untangled/2008/rest-apis-must-be-hypertext-driven).

@alexpetros you are using a common CRUD style "pretty URL" approach to URI design which I do not think Roy Fielding ever prescribes or requires. How the URI looks, or the hierarchies it is suggestive of, should not matter to the outside world, as the client in the REST model would be using links in the headers / content of the response as options for moving one resource to the next.
